116
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.120
So how do we start to put guardrails in effect on projects?

117
00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.840
And here's an example.

118
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.320
You could just say to your agent fix all the issues on this repo.

119
00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.640
And then it would just start throwing up pull requests

120
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.080
generating thousands and thousands of lines of code.

121
00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.400
And really the human you don't have to think about it.

122
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.960
There's no cost zero accountability, which opens up another can of worms.

123
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.960
So here's an example I work for for Google as a developer advocate.

124
00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.360
I've been in open source for the past five years at Google, working on Google

125
00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.720
ADK, so Agent Development Kit and most recently Gemini CLI.

126
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.720
And here's kind of just an example of some of the pull requests that we see

127
00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:42.800
where you have absolutely gibberish in the actual title.

128
00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.840
They we have pull request templates that we expect to try

129
00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.440
to help guide how you should be putting up code changes to the repository.

130
00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:51.920
They didn't fill any of them in.

131
00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.600
And if you look in the top right corner it's over 30,000 lines of code changed.

132
00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:01.840
So that is not going to pass the test.

133
00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.320
And so here's a quick example

134
00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.560
of how quickly you can actually go ahead and just spin up an agent.

135
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:09.360
We're going to say create.

136
00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.880
This is Gemini CLI, one of our products.

