00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.240
And we'll say create ten sub agents in parallel to go ahead

138
00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.800
and just create nonsensical pull requests to this repository.

139
00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.640
And we'll quickly see how in a matter of about 30, 45

140
00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.440
seconds, we're going to have ten pull requests up against this repository.

141
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.120
We didn't say what features to build.

142
00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.240
It's just going to make up random stuff.

143
00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.680
So you can see it's making a culinary slop.

144
00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:37.560
Chef we've got a bribe laundering engineer I have no idea what that is.

145
00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.680
And then it's going to go ahead and actually start

146
00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:41.240
putting up these pull requests.

147
00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.200
So this is kind of the open source world that we've now come to.

148
00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.640
Right? People can throw things.

149
00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:49.920
They can just absolutely create pull requests at a whim.

150
00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.040
So how do as maintainers, as stewards

151
00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.440
of the open source community, how do we fight this?

152
00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:00.160
So we'll let that run for a few seconds, and then we'll prove that there are ten

153
00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.400
pull requests up.

154
00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.320
So really this

155
00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:07.760
comes down to maintaining code isn't free.

156
00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.360
Generating code is free like we talked about, but maintaining it is not.

157
00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.120
And that's really kind of where this battle is now being fought.

158
00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:21.840
So GitHub release that over 45 million pull requests were merged

159
00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.080
per month in 2025.

160
00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.800
Like we said, how fast AI is evolving this number is probably,

161
00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.120
you know, super, super out of date.

162
00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.920
I think last I heard, GitHub has been in the news

163
00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:36.000
a lot recently for like trying to keep up with the pace of AI

164
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:36.920
and all the code generated.

165
00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:40.880
I think last month they said it was over 200 million pull requests.

166
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.640
So we're already seeing five x right there.

167
00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.000
And so this leads to a lot of issues.

168
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.440
We've seen multiple different companies already starting to face this.

169
00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:56.400
So curl actually had a basically a swarm of AI agents and bots creating

170
00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.280
fake security reports to the point where they were actually getting

171
00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.360
flagged by all of the systems and caused them a ton of headache.

172
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.160
We've seen other companies

173
00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.240
put in their contributions in their open source repositories

174
00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.120
that they just will not take anything that is pure AI generated.

175
00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.040
So 100% generated.

176
00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.600
And then there's also closed external pull requests.

177
00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.120
So essentially it's gotten so bad that they've just said

178
00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.840
we're doing it ourselves. We're not going to take anything.

179
00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.760
And then ghosty is an interesting one which has this

180
00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:30.080
based system which we'll talk about a bit more in a few minutes.

181
00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.520
So what are the main issues?

182
00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:38.160
Breaking down the barriers with AI should be a win, right?

183
00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.760
Makes it easier for people to contribute to these projects.

184
00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:43.320
But what's the flip side.

185
00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.560
And this is one that I've seen a lot and it's the drive by PR, right?

186
00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:50.600
So you have you run using a library or a framework or whatever

187
00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.920
it may be, and something doesn't go wrong.

188
00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.920
You say, oh, I've got the error message.

189
00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.600
Let me just go and fix this.

190
00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.480
You know, I know it's an open source project.

191
00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.480
Let me go and contribute to this.

192
00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:02.680
So you just throw in your stack trace,

193
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:05.760
you clone the repository and ask your agent to fix it.

194
00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.840
So that takes like 30s on your end.

195
00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:12.040
But the trick side and we'll assume that this is actually good code generated.

196
00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:14.680
We'll assume that for this scenario.

197
00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:17.440
Then you've got the maintainer who actually has to go and review that.

198
00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.400
So they're going to take their time, at least at Google.

199
00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.840
We still kind of hand review each PR that goes in.

200
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.000
We have like code reviews that are automated.

201
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.080
And we'll review the code agent first, but then it still gets to a point

202
00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.080
where a maintainer looks at the code and ships it

203
00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.680
with all the security vulnerabilities and everything.

204
00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.520
Right now that has been at scale, we make sure that humans see everything.

205
00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.880
So then they go and they post some feedback,

206
00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.120
you know, hey, maybe you change these two things and

207
00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:48.440
the drive by

208
00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:51.680
PR really is someone prompted it, but they had no expectation

209
00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:54.520
that they were actually going to have to follow up or do anything.

210
00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:54.800
Right.

211
00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.600
So it might have been a good first judgment being like, hey,

212
00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:00.720
I can try to fix this, but they're not going to come back.

213
00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.120
So you just have a prompt and forget it.

214
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:06.960
Where now it's either the maintainer needs to come and kind of take over that

215
00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:11.120
pull request, and it just kind of floats away into the abyss.

216
00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:17.000
The second one is agents like we showed in that quick example of ten sub agents.

217
00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:20.040
It's super easy to paralyze work.

218
00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.800
Unfortunately, we have not figured out how to do that as humans

219
00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:28.360
for our own brains, so we might be able to create code super, super fast.

220
00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:33.040
But the burden on the maintainer if we spin up ten pull requests in a minute.

221
00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.520
It's not going to take 10s to review all that code generated.

222
00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:38.000
So there's a disconnect there.

223
00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.080
And trust is somewhat breaking down.

224
00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.200
Like I mentioned, you don't necessarily know whether or not

225
00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:50.280
a human is on the other side of that code, whether it was even looked at.

226
00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.920
So when someone puts up a pull request, normally you would have gone back

227
00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:58.320
and forth, hey, why did you make this design decision nowadays?

228
00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:02.440
I don't know when I told me to or Claude told me to.

229
00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:06.960
So now the thing is not actually trust there.

230
00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.560
You can't actually assume that whoever put up that code even knows

231
00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.200
what's going on.

232
00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.080
So what can we do now?

233
00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:16.760
What are the new rules of open source?

234
00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:19.760
And how can we kind of fight these issues.

235
00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.440
And again, this will be kind of our experience on how

236
00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.040
we actually implement things for Gemini CLI, one of our open source repositories

237
00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:32.080
with 100,000 GitHub stars and over 700 external contributors.

238
00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.480
So quite a big scale. We see a lot of this stuff.

239
00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.160
The first one is actually pretty trivial.

240
00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:41.320
It's that we require you to put up a issue first before

241
00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:42.840
you can put up a pull request.

242
00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:46.320
So this kind of eliminates 90% of those drive by

243
00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:50.800
where, hey, I'm just going to try to fix this, put up a pull request.

244
00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.800
And then we actually close that and say, hey, can you put up an issue first?

245
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:59.360
So right there, if this is an agent or someone's bot,

246
00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:00.720
they're not going to come back.

247
00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:04.200
And most of the time will not go ahead and create that issue.

248
00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:06.280
Whereas if you're a human on the other side,

249
00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:09.120
you know, you are actually going to take the time to maybe go and file

250
00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.120
that and then start working on it, and then we'll take a look at your PR.

251
00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:14.160
So open the issue.

252
00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:16.400
And we normally try to have a maintainer approve that.

253
00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:19.040
Like if it's something that's going to be pretty hefty we'll go back and forth.

254
00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.240
Hey what are you thinking on the design okay.

255
00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:22.120
That looks good.

256
00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.760
Go ahead and start working on that.

257
00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:27.360
And that solves about probably 90% of the issues right there.

258
00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.680
That drive by PR is now kind of eliminated.

259
00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.160
There's kind of a buy in for that pull request.

260
00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:33.440
If they're going to put that up.

261
00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.440
They've kind of done their work, done their due diligence.

262
00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:39.080
The second one is kind of rate limit contributions.

263
00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:42.840
So for external contributors, we actually kind of put limits on

264
00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:45.960
how many pull requests they can have active at any given time.

265
00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:47.720
So for that example we showed where we just started

266
00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.680
generating ten different nonsensical ones.

267
00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.120
This would actually get stopped by that.

268
00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:54.400
And it's pretty easy to do in GitHub actions.

269
00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:58.360
But luckily just recently GitHub

270
00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.480
actually is now rolling out this feature by default.

271
00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:03.560
So you can actually go into your GitHub repository settings

272
00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:06.920
and actually set a limit for external contributors

273
00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:10.840
to how many active pull requests they can have any given time.

274
00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:12.360
And regular maintainers

275
00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.600
or people who are allowed to contribute to the project are not affected by this.

276
00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:19.360
So it's kind of a nice way where you don't, you know, go to sleep

277
00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.560
at night, wake up the next morning and be like, oh, I've got 40.

278
00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:27.240
You know, people trying to rewrite an entire library overnight.

279
00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.480
And the next one is automation.

280
00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.240
If there's going to be automation for generating the code,

281
00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.280
you should probably implement automation to help fight that.

282
00:14:36.280 --> 00:14:39.400
And this can be done in a way where you're not not necessarily

283
00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:40.560
even using AI, right.

284
00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.240
It can just be through GitHub actions.

285
00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:46.560
So we have some on our repository where it actually will check to see, hey,

286
00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.120
we followed up with somebody. Have they responded?

287
00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:53.040
If not after 60 days, which is probably a little generous,

288
00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:55.120
it'll go ahead and actually close out that issue.

289
00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:58.920
So a way of flagging things as stale or not really active.

290
00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:02.000
There's not the buy in on the other side because it is a two way street.

291
00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:06.720
And then some more examples or what you can do to kind of help fight.

292
00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:10.360
This is check in context files into your repository,

293
00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:13.680
and agents are supposed to read instructions

294
00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:16.080
whether they actually read the instructions.

295
00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.200
That's a different ball game. But we can do our best.

296
00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:21.280
And so the thing I'll also say is

297
00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:24.520
don't just put into into the repository.

298
00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:27.440
Don't just commit the context files for cloud.

299
00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.480
If you're using Claude, that's perfect.

300
00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:32.600
But you should actually add an agent as well.

301
00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:33.240
Because even if

302
00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.920
your team is using cloud for development, there might be people who are, you know,

303
00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.720
there's so many different tools out there that they might be using

304
00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.000
a different tool for development.

305
00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.640
So by putting in the other context files in there as well, or through a symlink,

306
00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:45.960
you can actually kind of make

307
00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:49.840
sure that the contributions of everyone are kind of uplifted and get better.

308
00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:51.640
And really it's pretty simple.

309
00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:55.400
You just throw in there like run the tests, follow the PR template.

310
00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:58.680
That's a big one, and it's actually makes quite a big difference.

311
00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:00.480
And you've heard

312
00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:04.200
a lot, I think about this over these two days about agent skills.

313
00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.480
Everyone is big on the skills right now.

314
00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:07.960
They're kind of blowing up everywhere.

315
00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:11.840
And they can also really help, not just uplift the quality from AI,

316
00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:14.360
but also from human people as well.

317
00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:17.320
So in our repository, we have a bunch of different skills

318
00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:22.080
that actually help kind of take this drive by or one off

319
00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:23.640
contributors and really raise the bar

320
00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.480
so they can actually help get their work merged.

321
00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:30.480
I think the biggest one I personally like a lot is the PR creator.

322
00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:31.480
You basically just say, hey,

323
00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:35.000
create a PR for this, it's going to go look at the template, make sure it's

324
00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.960
looking at the code that's changed, run the tests and do all that formatting.

325
00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:42.480
So if someone who is also contributing to the project says, hey,

326
00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.640
put up a pull request, it'll go through those exact same steps

327
00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:49.240
that normally someone, a human or maintainer would also do.

328
00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:53.160
It's kind of codifying those skills that you as a human would do

329
00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:54.360
into the code base.

330
00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.240
The other one is for docs.

331
00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:01.160
Nobody likes writing docs, but they are so important, especially,

332
00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.920
you know, to train the models and to actually have the models kind of

333
00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:06.920
look at the docs is a really big uplift and skill.

334
00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:10.720
So of course we have a docs writer skill to enforce the style guide and make sure

335
00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:14.960
that any features or code that's getting changed has docs going with it.

336
00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:17.960
Code review I'll skip over, but there's a lot of others

337
00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.080
and really checking these into the repositories

338
00:17:20.080 --> 00:17:22.280
so that not only your team members get the benefits,

339
00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.320
but also external folks is a really good skill.

340
00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:28.040
And we'll take an example of just kind of what this looks like.

341
00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.760
So here's a PR of mine actually that's gone through the PR creator skill.

342
00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.960
I also have my own personal skill that will like use playwright

343
00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:35.720
to take videos

344
00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:39.280
and screenshots of code changes as well so that you can actually have visual,

345
00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:40.680
visual proof.

346
00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.280
I find as a maintainer,

347
00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:46.200
if I see a screenshot or a video in a pull request, it's in my brain already.

348
00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:50.040
Hire a higher level of easier to see the code that's changed,

349
00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.880
as well as kind of somewhat knowing that there's been thought into it.

350
00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:58.360
So that's a little bit of what we've been doing at Google in our repositories.

351
00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.400
But what are others doing in the open source community?

352
00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.960
Let's take a look.

353
00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:05.880
Raise of hands that anyone heard of open

354
00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:09.680
source vacation as as a concept. No.

355
00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:10.880
Okay. Okay.

356
00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:13.520
So it is essentially think about it.

357
00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:15.800
When you go out of office, you probably put in

358
00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:21.080
like an automated email that says, hey, I'll be back at this day two weeks.

359
00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:24.840
Or if you really need something urgent, follow up with my boss.

360
00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:28.360
It's kind of the same concept, but for an open source project,

361
00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:32.400
if the team has a conference like this and we're all going to it,

362
00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:36.080
we can put it into our code base and essentially any pull requests

363
00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:40.680
or issues during that time, we'll get automatically closed and say, hey,

364
00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.120
you can put the message saying, hey,

365
00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:44.480
we're on vacation, we're on open source vacation.

366
00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:46.760
It's also another way that open source maintainers,

367
00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:49.880
if you're a solo maintainer and you're actually going out of office,

368
00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:51.920
you can do the same thing so that when you get back from your trip,

369
00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:56.320
you're not automatically swarmed with hundreds of pull requests or issues.

370
00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:58.280
So it's a really cool way to basically say,

371
00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.320
hey, we're not accepting contributions right now.

372
00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:02.360
We're coming back in like two days.

373
00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:06.240
And you can configure it to

374
00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:09.880
have the exact message as well as when it ends.

375
00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:15.040
And the Pi coding agent Mario over there has been a big user of this.

376
00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:18.680
He actually will go on really extended periods of OSS

377
00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.800
even when he's not not not out of office, but

378
00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:24.840
just doesn't want to accept any new contributions and essentially

379
00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.760
be able to get through the backlog, bring it down to zero and then he'll open.

380
00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:32.440
Open it up and gets flooded with more things and then vouch.

381
00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.840
Has anyone heard of vouch?

382
00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.800
Okay a few if you have heard of vouch.

383
00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:38.280
So vouch is a system for open source.

384
00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:40.720
That's kind of a referral system.

385
00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:44.640
So if someone's trying to get a job, they say, hey, I know Jack.

386
00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:45.800
He can vouch for me.

387
00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:48.800
It's the same concept, but for open source.

388
00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:53.080
So trusted maintainers or contributors can vouch for other people

389
00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:57.040
and essentially say, you know, hey, I know Macy is a good worker.

390
00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.240
She's done good code. I'm going to vouch for her.

391
00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.720
Now. She can put up pull requests and contribute to the project,

392
00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:06.920
and you can set the system defaults for like unknown people who are new.

393
00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:08.400
So maybe they have some permissions,

394
00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:12.280
but once you get a vouch, you have more access.

395
00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.880
And then the flip side you can actually get denounced.

396
00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:18.720
So bots or people who have put up sloppy code

397
00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:22.040
can get denounced denounced, and then they get blocked.

398
00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.640
And that's kind of similar to the OS.

399
00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:28.280
It uses markdown.

400
00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:30.400
This uses a TD file.

401
00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:33.280
So you just check it into your repo GitHub actions will scan it.

402
00:20:33.280 --> 00:20:37.680
And essentially is just a list of the GitHub handles

403
00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.200
where there's a list for the bad people, a list for the good people.

404
00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:44.680
So stay off the naughty list.

405
00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:46.440
And this is from Mitchell Hashimoto.

406
00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.000
So he created ghosty.

407
00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.920
He was also one of the founders of Terraform and HashiCorp.

408
00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:53.520
So he's been using it in ghosty,

409
00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:55.880
which is a pretty big open source library for a few months.

410
00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:57.640
And what's actually surprising to me is he said

411
00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:00.800
PR quality has gone way up by the voucher system,

412
00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:04.240
but he actually is still seeing the PR, so it's really just filtering out

413
00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:07.720
the bad ones so that he can get more of the good ones merged in.

414
00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:09.960
So I find that pretty interesting.

415
00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:14.560
This is, this is I haven't swung all the way to this side of the,

416
00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:15.680
of the, of the aisle yet.

417
00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:19.520
But some people are saying, you know, just don't accept issues at all.

418
00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:22.360
And now prompts are the new pull requests.

419
00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.520
So don't give me the code.

420
00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.760
Just give me the prompt that you use to generate the code

421
00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:28.080
and I'll go do it myself.

422
00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.440
So give me all the details.

423
00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:32.400
I need to basically go and fix this.

424
00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:34.080
I haven't swung all the way there yet.

425
00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:38.400
I think that's a little bit harsh, but it is being used by several

426
00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.160
big companies out there.

427
00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:43.320
So don't don't give me the output, give me the intent behind it.

428
00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:44.920
And we heard that in the keynote this morning.

429
00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.320
You know, it's moving on to intent and context.

430
00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:49.640
So give me the context. Give me the intent.

431
00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:53.680
And if it's right I will go and generate that code using your prompt.

432
00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.960
So where do we go from here?

433
00:21:57.960 --> 00:22:00.240
I think there's kind of like a new playbook that we're seeing evolved.

434
00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:01.080
Nobody really knows.

435
00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:03.720
We haven't really settled on. People are trying different things.

436
00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:07.120
There's vouch, there's open source vacation, different automations.

437
00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:11.080
Some people are even just leaving open source because of AI and many bots.

438
00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:14.040
But I think there's kind of a new playbook being written.

439
00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.800
And for us, at least for maintainers, it's, you know, create issues.

440
00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.800
First, try to set up external contribution limits

441
00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:23.440
so that you can kind of filter out the noise, you know, add the context

442
00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:27.440
and skills to kind of uplift all of the agents out there and then try

443
00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:30.840
these other systems like automation and trust through vouch.

444
00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:34.560
And on the flip side for the contributors, try to discuss your code,

445
00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:38.320
actually understand what is being written before you just put a pull request.

446
00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:42.480
There's still a little bit of a standard there and be open to respond to feedback.

447
00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:43.520
If you're going to try to,

448
00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:47.920
you know, add code to a code base, expect it to be a little bit of pushback.

449
00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:51.840
Try to learn something and try to be able to actually explain the decision

450
00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:52.880
behind that code.

451
00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:57.520
So the future is really moving towards collaboration.

452
00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:59.440
It's not just humans, it's not just agents.

453
00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:02.480
It's really a blend of the two, and that's where things are going.

454
00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:04.160
So there still needs to be trust.

455
00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:06.560
We still kind of need. And that's what vouch tries to do. Right.

456
00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:07.440
Try to have some trust.

457
00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:10.880
I still use AI to generate code, but there's always got to be the intent there

458
00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:14.960
and understanding on still on the system while you're making those decisions

459
00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:17.080
and then structure.

460
00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:20.080
So try to add these agent skills context files

461
00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:23.280
so that things can scale through humans and and bots

462
00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:25.560
and then respect.

463
00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.640
I think open source has always been on respect.

464
00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:33.680
And that's becoming even more crucial now that it's so easy to generate code,

465
00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:37.040
kind of be patient with maintainers, explain yourself.

466
00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.280
And that's the biggest thing.

467
00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:43.600
So what would I would take away from this is update your contributing files.

468
00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:47.440
Normally you would put how for humans to actually go ahead and interact

469
00:23:47.440 --> 00:23:48.240
with your code.

470
00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:50.800
I would actually add a section to look on.

471
00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:54.120
What is your contribution guidelines for agents or for AI.

472
00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:56.400
This will actually also weed out.

473
00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:59.120
Some agents will actually see this and be like, hey, I can't do this.

474
00:23:59.120 --> 00:23:59.560
Not really.

475
00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:01.600
They don't follow instructions that well yet,

476
00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:04.600
but it is kind of good to have that written down.

477
00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:08.240
And like we said, check in the files and check in the context files

478
00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:09.680
and skills into your

479
00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.560
and check in context files that aren't

480
00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:16.000
just for the tools you're using, but also that the global ones like agents,

481
00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:19.440
so that people who are using other tools, there's

482
00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:22.680
so many different tools out there can also benefit from it,

483
00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:26.920
and then add automation to protect your own time.

484
00:24:26.920 --> 00:24:30.640
If there's going to be automation through these bots and through all these agents,

485
00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:34.320
automate some of the easy stuff away, like that stale PR stuff we looked at,

486
00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:37.360
as well as PR reviews and things like that.

487
00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:39.080
